  "observed_rules": [
    "Moving a cut across units 16..17 can leave VTA internal DMA identical: topology A and C have exactly the same LOAD/STORE counters even though their framework boundary payload differs.",
    "A residual boundary can increase framework materialization without increasing VTA internal work: topology B returns two tensors and 301056 bytes per frame versus topology A's one tensor and 100352 bytes.",
    "Splitting one VTA region into two physical-stage invocations adds a second host-to-device boundary and single-VTA mutex delay. Topology D has 25 percent more host-to-device bytes than B and 20.337 ms median VTA mutex wait.",
    "Topology D has nearly the same LOAD call count and device wait as B but 29.30 percent more LOAD payload and 49.61 percent more weight LOAD payload, so call count alone cannot represent repeated transfer demand.",
    "Across these four historical high-performance topologies, FPS stays near 10.3 to 11.0 while boundary and DMA demand vary. Costs off the bottleneck can be hidden; pruning must combine single-VTA service, CPU stages, boundary bytes, and DDR demand."
